{
  "0": "Network architecture: add LayerNorm only to the GRU candidate-state preactivation before tanh, preserving reset/update gate equations, hidden width and depth. Stabilizing candidate activations may improve recurrent optimization without increasing recurrent depth. Keep embeddings, inputs, scoring, readout, loss and optimizer fixed. Apply across all four categories, training one model per category; update the predictor writer to reproduce the modified cell.",
  "1": "Training strategy: halve the current learning rate throughout training, leaving optimizer type, batch size, dropout, weight decay and scheduler timing unchanged. Electronics validation peaks early while training loss continues falling; smaller updates may preserve useful recurrent representations and improve generalization. Keep architecture, features, readout, scoring and loss fixed, with seed 42 and epoch cap 200. Train one model per category across all four categories.",
  "2": "Interest aggregation: replace the current readout with 0.8 times the last valid GRU state plus 0.2 times a masked recency-weighted mean of valid states, using weights proportional to exp(-(L-1-t)/5). This inexpensive readout may retain recent intent while reducing sensitivity to the final interaction. Preserve history selection, embeddings, GRU topology, scoring, loss and optimizer. Use identical causal pooling during training and prediction; retain one model per category across all four categories.",
  "3": "Embedding representation: apply affine-free RMS normalization to each nonpadding input item embedding before it enters the GRU, using epsilon 1e-6 and keeping padding zero. More consistent input magnitudes may prevent embedding norms from dominating recurrent gates, particularly in sparse Electronics histories. Preserve embedding dimensions, candidate scoring embeddings, GRU topology, features, history policy, readout, loss and optimizer. Train one model per category across all four categories and update the predictor writer."
}
